    expectedAnswer: "TypeScript adds static analysis to JavaScript. The key interview skill is explaining what the compiler can prove, what gets erased, what JavaScript runs at runtime, and how the type design scales in production.",
    deepExplanation: "Example:\n\n```ts\ninterface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nfunction greet(user: User): string {\n  return `Hello ${user.name}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet({ id: \"u1\", name: \"Rasik\" }));\n```\n\nProduction pattern:\n\n```ts\nconst count: number = 42;\n// const invalid: number = \"42\";\n// Error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.\n```\n\nWhy it matters: Improves correctness before runtime. Makes APIs and component contracts explicit. Supports refactoring and large-team collaboration. Provides architectural documentation through types.",
    productionExample: "```ts\nconst count: number = 42;\n// const invalid: number = \"42\";\n// Error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.\n```",
    bestPractices: ["Enable strict compiler settings.","Prefer `unknown` over `any` at trust boundaries.","Model domain invariants explicitly.","Keep shared types close to their owning domain.","Separate transport DTOs from domain models when transformations are needed."],
    tradeOffs: "Advantages: Stronger contracts and safer refactoring.; Better editor tooling.; Excellent fit for large React codebases.. Disadvantages: Type complexity can slow the compiler.; Types do not validate runtime data.; Poorly designed abstractions can become difficult to understand..",
    commonMistakes: ["Assuming TypeScript validates JSON at runtime.","Overusing `as` assertions.","Using `any` to silence compiler errors.","Creating excessively clever type-level abstractions.","Interview trap: Confusing compile-time types with runtime values.","Interview trap: Saying interfaces exist in emitted JavaScript.","Interview trap: Assuming all generic information exists at runtime.","Interview trap: Treating TypeScript as a replacement for runtime validation."],
